# id <TAB> upstream <TAB> meios <TAB> reason. The bidirectional reviewed divergence manifest:
# a divergence not listed here fails the differential run, a listed entry whose divergence no
# longer reproduces also fails it, and a listed entry present under the right id but with
# drifted recorded text fails it too -- matched by exact text, never by id alone. A document probe
# that refuses is recorded as REFUSED followed by the diagnostic code it gives, upstream's mapped
# through tests/refusal_causes.h. An expression probe's refusal stays REFUSED. No refusal's
# wording is compared, on either side.
div_self_reference	[1, [1, [...]]]	REFUSED	here a value never contains itself, so an alias inside the node it names refuses where the document is read, while an anchor reused by aliases outside that node loads; upstream builds a value that contains itself
div_dict_pair_sequence	{'a': 1}	REFUSED	the mapping constructor is admitted in its keyword-argument spelling alone, so a pair-sequence argument refuses here rather than taking a meaning nothing measured
div_dict_mixed_arguments	{'a': 1, 'b': 2}	REFUSED	a positional argument mixed with keyword arguments refuses for the same reason the pair-sequence argument does
div_string_repetition	ababab	REFUSED	a string repeated by an integer renders upstream; only the addition of two strings was measured into this grammar, so the multiplication operator refuses a string rather than taking a meaning nothing measured
div_string_ordering	True	REFUSED	upstream orders two strings by their code points; this grammar compares strings for equality alone, so an ordering operator against a string refuses rather than inventing a collation
div_string_split_whitespace	['a', 'b']	REFUSED	the no-argument split collapses runs of whitespace and drops the leading and trailing fields, a different algorithm from the explicit-separator form admitted here, so it refuses rather than being approximated by a single-space separator
div_string_split_limit	['a', 'b c']	REFUSED	a second argument bounds how many separators are consumed; one named operation taking one separator is admitted, so any further argument refuses
div_absolute_value	REFUSED	3	upstream's expression globals carry the mathematics module and the two reducers and no builtins at all, so the absolute value refuses there by name while this grammar answers it; it runs in the direction where this side accepts what upstream refuses, and it is the only row in that direction whose cause is the evaluator's own function set -- the two span rows that run the same way refuse upstream because the reference's span pattern truncates the span before its evaluator ever reads it. It is recorded rather than removed because the function is documented, implemented and range-checked, and dropping it would break a description this project already loads
div_span_closing_brace_in_literal	REFUSED	a}b	upstream's span pattern is quote-blind, so a closing brace inside a string literal ends the span there and the truncated fragment refuses as an unterminated literal; the close finder here folds over quotes and the whole literal renders, accepted by maintainer override on 2026-08-12, the day that fold landed
div_span_format_operator	1.234	REFUSED	the remainder operator formats a string against its right operand upstream; only arithmetic remainder was measured into this grammar, so a string operand refuses rather than taking a meaning nothing measured
div_span_nested_expression	REFUSED	3	upstream's span pattern never counts a nested brace, so an expression span written inside another ends at the inner brace and the truncated fragment refuses; the close finder here counts depth and the inner scan resolves the nested span before the outer expression is evaluated
non_text_mapping_key	<robot name="probe"><link name="from_number"></link></robot>	REFUSED expression_error	a mapping an auxiliary document keys by a number or a boolean loads on both sides, and upstream subscripts such a key by writing the key itself; every subscript key here is text, so the number reaches no entry and the document refuses rather than reading one. The refusal is raised by the subscript, not by the document parse, so this row records that the key is admitted and unreadable rather than rejected
argument_without_default	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	<robot name="probe"><link name="x"></link></robot>	here a declaration without a default declares a required argument, which binds nothing until a caller supplies it, and a use no caller supplied refuses naming the argument, while upstream refuses every argument declaration that carries no default
argument_helper_call	<robot name="probe"><link name="Aarm"></link></robot>	REFUSED unsupported_expression	upstream exposes a helper reading an argument by name inside an expression, which this grammar does not carry, so the call refuses as an unsupported call target
include_parent_directory	<robot name="probe"><link name="parent_leaf"></link></robot>	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	upstream honors a relative include filename that climbs out of the directory of the file writing it, while here a relative include climbing above its document's directory refuses as climbing above the directory of the document that writes it, a containment boundary this project keeps
include_absolute_path	<robot name="probe"><link name="absolute"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream reads an include whose filename is an absolute path, while here an absolute spelling is looked for only through the configured sources, with its leading separator removed, and refuses where none holds it
dirname_command	REFUSED unknown_substitution	<robot name="probe"><link name="dirname_command.d"></link></robot>	upstream hands its directory command no file and refuses it everywhere, while here it names the directory of the file it is written in
include_outside_link	<robot name="probe"><link name="linked_part"></link></robot>	REFUSED uncontained_asset	upstream reads a file beside the including document wherever a link there points, while here a link whose target leaves every directory that admits it (the one the including document was named in, the one it lives in, and each configured package root) refuses unless the caller follows outside links, a containment boundary this project keeps by default
include_package_filename	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="package_include"></link></robot>	upstream joins a package:// include filename onto the directory of the file writing it as a relative path and refuses where nothing is there, while here it is resolved through the configured sources as a package-qualified include
find_relative_root	<robot name="probe"><link name="True"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="False"></link></robot>	upstream's package lookup joins a relative configured root as written, so the directory it locates keeps the relative spelling, while here every configured root is made absolute before a lookup and the located directory is absolute; the probe compares whether the located directory equals the relative spelling, because an absolute path is not a fixed text
find_dotted_root	<robot name="probe"><link name="True"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="False"></link></robot>	upstream's package lookup joins a configured root as written, so a current-directory element in the root stays in the directory it locates, while here a configured root's current-directory elements are removed before a lookup, resolving no link, and the located directory carries none; the probe asks whether the located directory carries that element, because the absolute root around it is not a fixed text
include_find_climbs_out_of_package	<robot name="probe"><link name="sibling_package_part"></link></robot>	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	upstream joins a find's package directory and the rest of an include filename as written, so a parent element after the find reads a file in a sibling package, while here the rest of the filename is a path inside the find's package and leaving the package refuses, the containment every package-qualified include keeps
include_glob_filename	<robot name="probe"><link name="include_glob_filename"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream expands an include filename holding a glob character to the files it matches and includes each of them, while here include filenames are not globbed, so the pattern is looked for as a file of that name and refuses where none is
include_optional_from_expression	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	here the optional attribute of an include is evaluated before it is read as a boolean, as every boolean attribute is, so an expression yielding true marks the include optional and a missing file is skipped with a note, while upstream reads the attribute as written and refuses one that is an expression
include_optional_nested_missing	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	here optional covers only the include's own file, so a file it reads that includes a missing file without optional refuses as any include does, a mistake this side reports, while upstream also skips a failure to read any file the included one includes and drops everything the included file held
include_find_answering_root_only	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="r2_c"></link></robot>	upstream answers a find with the first root that holds the package and reads every file under that one directory, so a file only a later root holds under the same package is not found, while here each root is asked in order for the file itself and the later root's copy is read
include_missing_element_before_parent	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="c"></link></robot>	upstream opens an include's filename as the operating system resolves it, so a directory that does not exist before a parent step fails to open, while here the parent step is folded lexically first and the file beside the document is read
include_missing_element_before_parent_nested	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="urdf_c"></link></robot>	upstream opens an include's filename as the operating system resolves it, so a directory that does not exist before a parent step fails to open, while here the parent step is folded lexically first and the file under the sibling directory is read
include_find_missing_element_before_parent	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="p_c"></link></robot>	upstream opens the path a find rendered as the operating system resolves it, so a directory that does not exist under the package before a parent step fails to open, while here the parent step is folded lexically first and the package's file is read
include_held_find_in_property	<robot name="probe"><link name="p_urdf_c"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream renders a find to a directory wherever it is written, so a property holding it composes an absolute filename that names the package's file, while here an absolute include filename is looked for only through the configured sources and the composed filename refuses
include_held_find_in_argument	<robot name="probe"><link name="p_urdf_c"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream renders a find to a directory wherever it is written, so an argument default holding it composes an absolute filename that names the package's file, while here an absolute include filename is looked for only through the configured sources and the composed filename refuses
include_held_find_directory	<robot name="probe"><link name="p_urdf_c"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream renders a find to a directory wherever it is written, so a property holding a find and a subdirectory composes an absolute filename that names the file under it, while here an absolute include filename is looked for only through the configured sources and the composed filename refuses
include_find_composed_reads_neighbor	<robot name="probe"><link name="purdf_c"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream renders a find to a directory and joins the text after it with no separator, so the filename names a file under a neighboring directory of the package root, while here a filename not under the package's own directory is looked for only through the configured sources and refuses
include_find_trailing_separator	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="p_urdf_c"></link></robot>	upstream opens the filename as written, so a trailing separator after a file fails as not a directory, while here the package's file is read despite the trailing separator
cwd_command	<robot name="probe"><link name="True"></link></robot>	REFUSED unknown_substitution	upstream renders the whole text cwd in a substitution as the working directory, which equals the stage the probe is handed as an argument, while here cwd is not a substitution command and refuses as unknown
eval_command_padded	REFUSED unknown_substitution	<robot name="probe"><link name="l1"></link></robot>	upstream recognizes eval only at the very start of a substitution, so a leading space makes the whole text an unknown command, while here the leading space is skipped and eval evaluates its expression
eval_command_empty	REFUSED unknown_substitution	REFUSED unsupported_expression	upstream recognizes eval only when a space follows it, so eval with nothing after it is an unknown command, while here it is read as the eval command and refuses as an expression the native evaluator does not support
eval_command_blank	REFUSED expression_error	REFUSED unsupported_expression	upstream hands the empty text after eval and its space to Python, which refuses it as a syntax error, while here the blank expression refuses as one the native evaluator does not support
env_command_two_words	REFUSED unresolved_env	<robot name="probe"><link name="lprobe_value"></link></robot>	upstream refuses an env substitution with a second word, while here the first word names the variable and the second is ignored
optenv_command_fallback_words	<robot name="probe"><link name="la b"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="la  b"></link></robot>	upstream joins the fallback words of an optenv substitution with single spaces, while here the fallback is the text after the variable name as written, its run of spaces included
empty_command	<robot name="probe"><link name="l$()"></link></robot>	REFUSED unknown_substitution	upstream keeps a substitution with no command as written, while here it refuses as an unknown substitution
yaml_empty_scalar_key	<robot name="probe"><link name="v"></link></robot>	REFUSED expression_error	upstream loads an explicit mapping key with no content as the null key and reads the document's other keys, while here that key is not read as a null key and the whole document refuses
property_infinity_spelling	<robot name="probe"><link name="linf"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="lINFINITY"></link></robot>	a real here is always finite, so a spelling of infinity keeps its text as written, while upstream reads it as infinity and renders inf
property_overflowing_real	<robot name="probe"><link name="linf"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="l1e400"></link></robot>	a real here is always finite, so a literal beyond the double range keeps its text as written, while upstream reads it as infinity and renders inf
conditional_real_one	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	<robot name="probe"><link name="t"></link></robot>	here a number written as text is read by its truth, so 1.0 is true and the branch renders, while upstream reads only integer text as a number in a condition, so 1.0 is not a boolean there and refuses
comment_kept_in_output	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments: <!-- kept -->	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments:	upstream carries every comment of the document into its output, while here comments are not parsed, so none reaches the expansion
attribute_under_conditional	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link a="1" name="l"></link></robot>	here an xacro:attribute whose parent is a conditional is placed on the element the conditional's content lands in, with a note saying so, while upstream sets it on the conditional itself, a node the output drops, so the attribute is lost
attribute_under_macro_body	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link a="1" name="l"></link></robot>	here an xacro:attribute written as a child of a macro's body is placed on the element holding the call, with a note saying so, while upstream sets it on the body's copy, which the output drops, so the attribute is lost
attribute_under_included_root	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link a="1" name="l"></link></robot>	here an xacro:attribute written as a child of an included document's root is placed on the element holding the include, with a note saying so, while upstream sets it on that root, which the output drops, so the attribute is lost
attribute_under_block_wrapper	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link a="1" name="l"></link></robot>	here an xacro:attribute inside the wrapper of a block inserted by its content alone is placed on the element the content is inserted into, with a note saying so, while upstream sets it on the wrapper, which the insertion drops, so the attribute is lost
comment_evaluated_under_eval_comments	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments: <!-- v -->	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments:	upstream drops a comment holding xacro:eval-comments and evaluates each comment after it until an element, non-empty text or the off marker, while here comments are not parsed, so none reaches the expansion
comment_before_xacro_element_removed	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments: <!-- b -->	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments:	upstream deletes the comments directly before an element of xacro's grammar and keeps those before any other element, while here comments are not parsed, so none reaches the expansion
processing_instruction_kept	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments: <?probe value?>	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments:	upstream carries every processing instruction of the document into its output, while here processing instructions are not parsed, so none reaches the expansion
include_glob_matching_nothing	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream expands an include filename holding a glob character, warns when the pattern matches no file and includes nothing, so the rest of the document renders, while here include filenames are not globbed and the pattern is looked for as a file of that name, which refuses where none is
include_namespace_not_text	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	REFUSED unsupported_expression	both sides evaluate an include's namespace before reading its file and refuse one written as a list, upstream because a list cannot key a table, and here because the bracketed list literal that names it is not an expression this evaluator reads
comment_evaluation_switched_off	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments: <!-- v --> <!-- ${x} -->	<robot name="probe"><link name="l"></link></robot> comments:	upstream evaluates each comment after one holding xacro:eval-comments and stops at one holding xacro:eval-comments:off, keeping the later comment verbatim, while here no comment is kept
find_without_package_index	REFUSED unknown_substitution	REFUSED unresolved_find	upstream reads a find through a package index the pinned install does not ship, so without one every find refuses as an unsupported substitution, while here a find no configured source answers refuses as unresolved
property_chain_deeper_than_depth_ceiling	<robot name="probe"><link name="l1"></link></robot>	REFUSED expansion_budget_exceeded	upstream renders a chain of properties each reading the next, bounded only by its interpreter's recursion limit, while here each nested property read is charged against the expression-depth ceiling, so a chain deeper than that ceiling admits refuses
include_namespace_read_as_value	<robot name="probe"><link name="a"></link></robot>	REFUSED expression_error	upstream reads an include namespace named bare as the table holding its names, so a condition over one that holds a name is true, while here a namespace has no value of its own and a bare read of it refuses, pointing at the dotted spelling that reads one of its names
sum_not_a_reference_global	REFUSED undefined_property	REFUSED unsupported_expression	upstream's expression globals bind no name sum, so the call refuses there as an undefined name, while here the built-in evaluator refuses the list the call is given as an expression it does not support, and the python evaluator binds sum among its builtins and renders the call
include_namespace_reads_global_constant	<robot name="probe"><link name="l2.718281828459045"></link></robot>	REFUSED undefined_property	upstream's expression globals hold the mathematics constants, so a name an include namespace does not bind falls through to e there and renders, while here the built-in evaluator binds pi alone among those constants, so the read through the namespace refuses as the bare e does, and the python evaluator renders it as upstream does
attribute_value_not_text	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	<robot name="probe"><link a="1" name="l"></link></robot>	here an xacro:attribute's evaluated value is written as its text, so ${1} sets 1, while upstream cannot write a value that is not text and refuses
